      ],
      "application_cycle": "closed/inactive",
      "next_deadline": "Not applicable. Replaced by the voluntary Regional Housing Incentive Program and Title 31 Regional Housing Fund adopted October 21, 2025.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "false",
      "layering_notes": "San Luis Obispo County's Department of Social Services Homeless Services Division (HSD, Devin Drake, Director; George Solis, Principal Administrative Manager; HSDgrants@co.slo.ca.us) runs one combined annual Action Plan NOFA each fall for CDBG, HOME, ESG, HHAP, PLHA, Title 29 and County General Fund Support on the Neighborly portal, and is the Continuum of Care collaborative applicant and administrative entity (CA-614). The Department of Planning and Building runs Title 22 land use incentives and assesses the new Title 31 in-lieu fee; the Behavioral Health Department holds BHSA; the Housing Authority of San Luis Obispo (HASLO) and its San Luis Obispo Nonprofit Housing Corporation, Paso Robles Housing Authority and Peoples' Self-Help Housing are the main developers; and the private nonprofit San Luis Obispo County Housing Trust Fund is the local CDFI gap lender. The Urban County covers the unincorporated area plus Arroyo Grande, Atascadero, Morro Bay, Paso Robles, Pismo Beach and San Luis Obispo (Grover Beach is outside). The County repealed its inclusionary ordinance on June 7, 2022 and replaced it in October 2025 with a voluntary Regional Housing Incentive Program and Regional Housing Fund. slocounty.ca.gov opens directly but rate-limits bursts (HTTP 429); Legistar web API is not enabled for the County. Market-rate projects in the unincorporated county carry no inclusionary obligation, which removes a layering source but also makes mixed-income deals simpler. Cities (San Luis Obispo, Paso Robles and others) keep their own inclusionary rules (Pass 3).",
      "source_url": "https://www.slocounty.ca.gov/departments/social-services/forms-documents/homeless-services/homepage/2026/title-29-report-for-fy-2025-26",
